# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash, and that matters: a bare `debug` matches *any*
# directory of that name at any depth, which silently swallowed
# `ide/gearbox-studio/src/browser/theia/debug/` -- a source directory named after
# the Theia package it overrides. The build kept working locally and a fresh
# clone would not have compiled. Cargo's own output is `target/debug`, and
# `target` is already ignored, so the unanchored form bought nothing.
/debug
target
/logs
# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb

# Generated by cargo mutants
# Contains mutation testing data
**/mutants.out*/

# Code coverage reports
/coverage/

# Local clone of the RustSec advisory DB, fetched by cargo-deny
# (see `[advisories].db-path` in deny.toml)
.cargo/advisory-db

# Resolved products and generated artifacts: process crates, configs,
# Dockerfiles, charts. Reproducible from product.lock, so not tracked.
/.gearbox

# Editors. Nothing IDE-specific is tracked -- see .editorconfig for the
# settings that are.
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store

# Gearbox Studio (ide/): npm tree, build output, tsc incremental cache.
# Two generated trees ARE tracked, because the anti-drift checks are `git diff`
# and so depend on git seeing them: ide/gearbox-studio/src/common/generated/
# (`make ts-check`) and ide/gearbox-studio/src/browser/gdl/generated/
# (`make grammar-check`).
node_modules/
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo

# tsc output of the Theia extension, and everything `theia build` generates.
# `src-gen/` and the three `*esbuild*.mjs` files are written by @theia/cli from
# browser-app/package.json on every build, so tracking them would mean a diff
# for anyone whose Theia version differs.
/ide/gearbox-studio/lib/
/ide/browser-app/lib/
/ide/browser-app/src-gen/
/ide/browser-app/esbuild.mjs
/ide/browser-app/gen-esbuild.*.mjs

# VS Code extensions fetched by `npm run plugins` -- 5.6 MB of somebody else's
# build output, and the whole directory including itself. Nothing is kept here,
# not even a .gitkeep: the plugin deployer treats every entry as a plugin
# candidate and warns about the ones it cannot unpack, so a placeholder file
# would put a warning in every startup log. Which versions and why is in
# ide/README.md, where documentation belongs.
/ide/plugins/

# Playwright's per-run artifacts: failure screenshots and traces. The
# conformance table itself IS tracked (docs/conformance.md) -- it is the
# generated replacement for a hand-maintained section of the plan, so it is only
# useful if a diff shows when a claim changed state.
/ide/test-results/
# The write-trace log the conformance guards read: `global-teardown` deletes it
# on a clean run, so it is only ever present after a run that was interrupted --
# exactly when nobody wants to be asked about it by `git status`.
/ide/tests/.write-traces.log
/ide/playwright-report/
/ide/blob-report/
/ide/.playwright-artifacts-*/

# Python virtualenvs and caches
/.venv/*
**/__pycache__

# Agent worktrees, created by the harness under .claude/worktrees/.
.claude/

# Local secrets. Never committed: this file holds API keys, and it is not
# tracked, so an unignored `.env` is one `git add -A` away from a live
# credential in the history.
# Unanchored on purpose: a root-anchored `/.env` missed `docs/.env`, which is
# exactly where one got written. These match at any depth.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
ide/tests/.description-watch.log

/ide/electron-app/lib/
/ide/electron-app/src-gen/
/ide/electron-app/*esbuild*.mjs
/ide/electron-app/.browser_modules/
/ide/.desktop-runtime/
